  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nRain this evening through Thursday: A round or two of rain is \nexpected beginning this evening through Thursday morning. Regional \nradar and surface observation show a north/south oriented band of \nrain across MN along and just a head of a surface boundary slowly \nmoving out of the northern Plains. This band of rain is expected to \nslowly push into central and north-central WI this evening as the \nsurface front and plume of Pacific moisture work east. On and off \nrain is then expected to continue through Wednesday as the front and \nweak isentropic ascent continue over the region. Weak elevated \ninstability (MUCAPE 100-200 J/kg) may lead to a few rumbles of \nthunder Wednesday, but no organized thunderstorms are expected. \nEnsemble guidance has trended lower in terms of QPF amounts through \nThursday morning with this system. Areas south of HWY 29 now having \na 20-30% chance for greater than 1\" of rain and areas north have \nabout 20-40% for greater than 0.5\" of rain.  \n\nExtended: Subsidence behind the front will usher in surface high \npressure late on Thursday, which should stick around for the better \npart of the extended. As such, expect a pattern shift to cooler and \ndrier weather, with the exception of 30 to 50% chance of rain on \nSaturday, highest across northern WI. With surface high sitting \noverhead and falling temperatures, conditions should be favorable \nfor effective radiational cooling and frost development at times \nduring the early morning periods throughout the week. Will continue \nto monitor trends for headline potential as we approach the end of \nthe growing season.",
